{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "heat_column_varcoeff_flux_bc_tracer",
    "description": "MMS driver for grids/column_nonuniform_1d_tracer/rules/varcoeff_face_laplacian_lev_flux_bc_tracer: the 1-D heat equation u_t = d/dz(K du/dz) solved for TWO tracers at once, u shaped [lev, tracer] with NTRACER = 2 bound at the rule's import edge, on the same smoothly stretched unit column as problems/heat_column_varcoeff_flux_bc (the grid's shared MMS column mms_column_ze, quadratic stretch z(xi) = 0.6 xi + 0.4 xi^2) with the same shared interface coefficient K = alpha. Tracer s carries its OWN manufactured solution u_s(z,t) = a_s z + b_s exp(-alpha pi^2 t) cos(pi z), a = (1, -2), b = (1, 0.5) (spelled a_s = 4 - 3s, b_s = 1.5 - s/2), and therefore its OWN prescribed fluxes kdudz_bot[s] = kdudz_top[s] = alpha a_s, observeds tied to alpha. Tracer 1 is exactly problems/heat_column_varcoeff_flux_bc; tracer 2 has the opposite-signed, doubled ramp and half the cosine amplitude. Because the operator is linear, column by column, and annihilates every linear ramp (each discrete interface gradient of a_s z is a_s, cancelled at the faces by the prescribed alpha a_s), tracer 1's error field is exactly the 1-D problem's and tracer 2's is exactly half of it, so the (relative, esm-spec 6.6.5) L2 error over the [lev, tracer] field falls at the 1-D problem's second order. A per-tracer mix-up (reading another tracer's u, or another tracer's face flux) breaks the ramp cancellation and gives an O(1) error. The per-tracer column totals sum_k u[k,s] dz[k] (the grid's tracer_column_total template) are conserved exactly by the discrete operator because each tracer's two face fluxes are equal; the test pins them at t = 0 and t = 0.1 to the same values, tracer 1's as the field max and tracer 2's as the field min (an element assertion on a shaped observed, total[s], is not evaluated by every binding). Build-time ic/reference positions spell the layer centre 0.5 (z((i-1)/NLEV) + z(i/NLEV)) inline from literals and NLEV (AGENTS.md build-time scope caveat).",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:column_nonuniform_1d_tracer",
      "equation:heat",
      "mms"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. J. Fluids Eng. 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      }
    ]
  },
